cc9 — the C/C++ toolchain for 9front (clang -> ld.lld -> a.out, on the box). Changelog.
(`pac9 changelog cc9` shows this; newest first.)

0.2.2 — 2026-07-26
  TIOCGWINSZ reports the terminal's CURRENT size, not the size it had at exec.

    - ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) read /env/LINES and /env/COLS, which is not a live
      channel to a child here: fork is rfork(RFPROC|RFFDG|RFENVG), so the child
      holds a COPY of the environment group from exec and never sees the
      terminal's later writes. Measured under alacritty9: after resizing
      1024x768 -> 620x660, a child's /env/COLS still read 113 while the
      terminal's live size file read "68 38". Every TUI that asks the runtime
      for its size therefore kept laying out at the startup width — lines wider
      than the window get their tails clipped, and a streaming redraw that
      counts rows for the wrong wrap width lands on the wrong rows and repeats
      them. Now reads $A9_SIZE_FILE first and falls back to /env.

0.2.1 — 2026-07-26
  The shipped archive is built OPTIMIZED, with word-wise mem*. 0.2.0 was packed from
  a tree five minutes older than that fix, so it still carried the -O0 runtime with
  byte-at-a-time memcpy/memset/memcmp/memchr — every malloc, syscall wrapper and
  string routine in every on-box-compiled binary. Measured on cirno with the on-box
  cc: memcpy over 4 MiB buffers now runs at ~3.9 GB/s.
  (poll.c, shm9.c, posix_llvm.c and pthread.c stay unoptimized on purpose — they
  share plain struct fields between peer threads, which an optimizer may cache in a
  register and spin on. Auditing that sharing is its own change.)
  Re-verified on the box: pollfork_gate 6/6 PASS, C++/threads/regex/filesystem/
  socket smoke 7/7 PASS, both compiled by the on-box cc.

0.2.0 — 2026-07-26
  The on-box toolchain catches up with three weeks of runtime work. 0.1.0 shipped a
  runtime archive built before the poll layer, the socket layer and the shared-memory
  layer existed, so on-box builds could not link a program that called poll(2) or
  socket(2) at all — the link just failed with "undefined symbol: poll". They link and
  run now, and every runtime fix below is in the archive.

    - poll(2), sockets and shared memory are IN the shipped archive (poll.o, net9.o,
      netcompat.o, shm9.o were simply absent from 0.1.0's libcc9cxx.a).
    - fork() no longer kills the PARENT's threads. A child gets a copy of the worker
      thread registry whose pids belong to the parent; a child that exits without
      exec'ing posted "kill" notes to all of them, so a program that spawned a helper
      lost its own poll/stdin threads while otherwise running fine.
    - Child reaping happens where Plan 9 allows it: await(2) in the forking proc, not
      /proc/<pid>/wait from a sibling thread (which delivers exactly one message and
      then blocks forever). waitpid() also decodes the native exit STRING instead of
      reporting the same status for every nonzero exit.
    - The poll ring no longer drops the tail of a transfer. Ring ownership keyed on a
      live reader thread, but the reader exits the instant the kernel gives it EOF —
      routinely with bytes still in the ring, which read() then discarded. A 220 KB HTTP
      body arrived as 4 KB, nondeterministically. Rings also grow on demand instead of
      pinning every fd at a fixed size, a timed poll() re-scans instead of sleeping
      through a wake, FIONREAD no longer starts a reader thread (that recursed into a
      stack overflow), and dup() carries O_NONBLOCK.
    - malloc/realloc are O(1). realloc bypassed every fast path, so each Vec growth was
      O(n) and a JS bytecode compile went O(n²).
    - Shared memory (#g) got a run of fixes: adopt an already-attached segment instead
      of failing "segments overlap", lazily fault-attach a sibling thread's mapping
      (RFMEM shares memory, not segment tables — this was a hard crash), reap zombie
      segments, bound idle pools by LRU, never detach on unmap, and real errnos out of
      segattach errstrs.
    - struct tm stays at its 36-byte ABI. Widening it made gmtime_r write 20 bytes past
      every tm a prebuilt library allocated — which silently broke certificate dates,
      and with them all HTTPS.
    - Writing to a closed pipe raises SIGPIPE instead of faulting; buffered writes are
      flushed before exit kills the drain threads; open(2) honours O_CLOEXEC; a real
      console counts as a tty and a spawned child keeps its stdio; RLIMIT_AS/RLIMIT_NPROC
      soft caps let a runaway program hit malloc failure instead of taking the box down;
      __stack_chk_fail prints the smashing PC.
    - Headers are refreshed from the tree (67 files, up from 39): sockets, netinet, poll,
      dirent d_type values, the full errno set, and the rest.

  Kept from 0.1.0 unchanged: the natively built clang, ld.lld, elf2aout, the `cc`
  driver, its cc1 template, and the libc++ header tree.

0.1.0 — 2026-07-01
  First release: clang + ld.lld + elf2aout running ON 9front, compiling ordinary C and
  C++ (exceptions, RTTI, iostreams, threads, regex, filesystem) to native a.out.
